## Migration: `Thread.Abort` → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why this breaks

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ (`SYSLIB0006`). `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone. The entire preemptive-abort model is removed.

### Pattern classification

Your code is a **timeout enforcement** pattern — it spawns a thread, waits with `Join(timeout)`, and calls `Abort()` if the deadline passes. The `catch (ThreadAbortException) { ResetAbort(); }` is dead code in modern .NET.

### Migrated code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation is *cooperative*. `Task.Run` won't kill the delegate — it keeps running in the background. If `work()` is a tight CPU loop, it won't stop unless it checks the token. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. It holds timers internally. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at this orchestration boundary. The `when` guard ensures you only catch *your* timeout, not an unrelated cancellation. |
| **Fire-and-forget background work** | After timeout, the abandoned `Task.Run` delegate continues consuming CPU/memory. For truly uncooperative code, move it to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()`. |

### If the work can cooperate (preferred)

Change the signature to accept a token and check it inside long-running logic:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}

// Caller example:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

This is the only way to guarantee the work actually stops on timeout.
